@startuml arch-importer-pipeline
' The Google Books importer end-to-end: one prefix file's journey from a remote .gz
' to a durable, MKN-annotated shard. Swimlanes separate the three concurrent actors
' (worker pool, storage layer, cron thread) so the ASYNCHRONY is visible, not implied.
' Colour legend (semantic, shared across all repo diagrams — see architecture.puml):
'   #1565C0 blue = statistical LM core   #00838F teal = storage / persistence
'   #006064 dark teal = the eviction tail  #546E7A grey = external / corpora
' Render: plantuml -tsvg docs/diagrams/arch-importer-pipeline.puml
' Source of truth for Figure 2 in docs/architecture/google-books-importer.md §3
skinparam backgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam ArrowColor #37474F
skinparam ArrowFontColor #37474F
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #FFF3E0
skinparam NoteBorderColor #EF6C00
skinparam activity {
  FontColor white
  FontStyle bold
  BorderColor #263238
  DiamondBackgroundColor #ECEFF1
  DiamondFontColor #263238
  DiamondBorderColor #455A64
}
title Importer pipeline — one prefix file, three concurrent actors

|#F3F5F7|Worker  (×W · tokio)|
start
:**Claim job** (order, prefix)
from the async job queue; <<#546E7A>>
:**Fetch** the .gz prefix file
HTTP stream — or, with --cache-files, download it
to local disk first and then stream from there; <<#546E7A>>
:**Gunzip** into lines; <<#546E7A>>
:**Parse** — zero-allocation
byte-scan for tabs, yielding a borrowed record whose
n-gram text points back into the line buffer; <<#1565C0>>
:**Aggregate years**
<latex>c(g) = \sum_{y \in Y} \text{match\_count}(g, y)</latex>
only one n-gram is buffered at a time, because the
files arrive sorted by n-gram; <<#1565C0>>

|#EDF3F8|Storage  (NgramStorage)|
:**Encode the key**
each token becomes a vocabulary index, then a LEB128
varint. The key is the concatenated varints, so no
delimiter character is ever needed; <<#00838F>>
:**Route to a shard**
by the hash of the first token (default),
or by that token's character prefix; <<#00838F>>

if (which write path?) then (prefix-based granularity)
  :**Chunked transaction**, SET semantics
  buffer each insert into a document transaction; <<#00838F>>
  if (buffered ≥ --tx-chunk-size?) then (yes)
    :**commit_and_renew_prefix_tx**
    flush this chunk to the WAL, then open a fresh
    transaction for the same prefix and shard; <<#00838F>>
  else (no)
  endif
  :**commit_prefix_tx**
  commit the final chunk **and** mark the prefix complete; <<#00838F>>
else (hash-based granularity — the default)
  :**Per-record increment_cas**
  a lock-free CAS straight into the shard's overlay,
  bounded by the flush threshold and the overlay
  budget rather than by a transaction; <<#00838F>>
endif

|#EBF3F3|Cron thread  (×1)|
:**Wake** every checkpoint_interval_ms; <<#546E7A>>
:**Rotate the vocabulary WAL first**
so that word indices are durable //before// any prefix
can be claimed complete; <<#00838F>>
:**Sync and checkpoint the shards** (rayon, ≤8 at once)
publishing each overlay snapshot into its durable image; <<#00838F>>
:**Eviction tail**
evict the coldest resident overlay nodes down to
<latex>\max(G / n_{\text{resident}},\ 64\,\text{MiB})</latex> — **losslessly**; <<#006064>>
:**Save the checkpoint metadata** — last, never first; <<#00838F>>

|#EDF3F8|Storage  (NgramStorage)|
:**Final vocabulary compaction**
one full image, written exactly once; <<#00838F>>
:**MKN aggregation** (rayon over the shards)
<latex>n_1 \ldots n_4 \Rightarrow Y, D_1, D_2, D_{3+}</latex>
together with <latex>N_{1+}(\bullet, w)</latex> and <latex>N_{1+}(w, \bullet)</latex>; <<#1565C0>>
:**Merge the shards** into the output trie
by <latex>O(\log N)</latex> parallel reduction; <<#00838F>>
stop

note right
  **A crash anywhere above just means re-running the prefix.**
  SET semantics make the re-insert idempotent, and any
  uncommitted chunk was never durable in the first place.
  Machine-checked by CheckpointStateMachine (DisjointSets,
  CrashRecoverySound) and by PersistentStorageBridge
  (ClaimRequiresDurableEvidence).
end note

@enduml
